WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:07.299
How many wars is the United States fighting
right now?

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:09.090
Well, one answer is zero.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:14.510
Under the Constitution, Congress must declare
war, and the last countries that Congress

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:19.539
declared war on were Bulgaria, Romania, and
Hungary, members of the Axis powers.

00:00:19.539 --> 00:00:21.310
That was in 1942.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:28.349
That’s right – the United States has not
declared war on any country since 1942.

00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:31.429
Eight decades, zero wars.

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:35.180
America must be one of the most peaceful countries
on earth!

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.309
But as you know, that’s not the reality.

00:00:37.309 --> 00:00:41.650
America is still fighting wars around the
world; it just doesn’t want to call them

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:42.809
wars.

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:48.080
In recent years, the United States has been
fighting in Afghanistan, Iraq, Libya, Pakistan,

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:53.079
Somalia, Syria, and Yemen – seven different
countries, and those are just the ones the

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:55.949
Pentagon officially acknowledges.

00:00:55.949 --> 00:00:59.899
The actual scope of American warmaking is
wider.

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:05.199
Researchers at Brown University recently counted
14 countries in which U.S. troops were engaged

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:07.500
in combat to fight terrorism.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.750
And then there are the forces the United States
stations indefinitely overseas, as part of

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.890
a massive network of allies and partners.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:21.800
Take the Middle East: U.S. troops number around
13,000 in Kuwait, another 13,000 in Qatar,

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.939
and 7,000 in Bahrain.

00:01:23.939 --> 00:01:27.380
Around 78,000 remain in Japan and South Korea.

00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:32.549
And that’s not even getting to Germany,
Djibouti, Cuba, Romania, and so on.

00:01:32.549 --> 00:01:38.600
In all, America stations about TWO HUNDRED
THOUSAND service members overseas, because

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:44.460
it has committed them to protect, by force,
about ONE THIRD of all the countries in the

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:45.490
world.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:50.049
Where does that leave the other two-thirds,
home to most of humanity?

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:52.750
The answer is on the receiving end.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:58.439
Over the last 20 years, the United States
has dropped an average of 46 bombs and missiles

00:01:58.439 --> 00:02:00.540
on other countries EACH DAY.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:07.930
In 2016, Barack Obama’s last year in office,
the United States dropped upwards of 26,000

00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:09.530
bombs on seven countries.

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:10.979
But not to worry.

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:14.420
America’s coercive power is not supposed
to be for “war.”

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:21.210
It’s for “military-led operations,”
or “police actions,” or “humanitarian

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.210
interventions.”

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:25.660
Bombings are “surgical strikes.”

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:29.580
And of course, there’s the “collateral
damage” – a term the U.S. military created

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:34.450
during the Vietnam War as a euphemism for
dead, innocent people.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:39.280
At least America used to have a Department
of War, calling it what it was.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:44.430
But in 1949, the “Department of Defense”
was born, and the American military establishment

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:50.610
began to pioneer a language of sterile obfuscation
to describe the brutal realities of war.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:54.120
The problem goes well beyond language.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.840
Over decades, America has PERFECTED the art
of isolating itself from the wars it wages.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:06.519
On TV, wars play out like simple dramas: us
versus them, with no blood and no casualties,

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:09.280
unless they’re inflicted by the enemy.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.580
Americans rarely see images of combat.

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:16.709
To judge from most broadcasts, war involves
firing missiles into the sky and admiring

00:03:16.709 --> 00:03:19.410
“the beauty of our weapons,” to quote
one anchor.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:26.680
And the PEOPLE America sends to war are increasingly
separated from the rest of society.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:32.080
Since the draft ended in the 1970s, the government
moved to an all-volunteer force, and relies

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:37.720
as much as possible on technology, like drones,
that keep American casualties to a minimum.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:42.510
So the wars America conducts take place largely
outside of public view.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:48.310
It was only when four U.S. soldiers were killed
in Niger in 2017 that many Americans learned

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:50.920
that WE HAD TROOPS THERE in the first place.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:56.870
Although the system perpetuates itself like
a self-licking ice cream cone, U.S. leaders

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:59.370
have a theory of the case.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:04.180
Military dominance, they say, is supposed
to achieve “peace through strength.”

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:10.240
This theory guided American leaders in the
1990s, after the Soviet Union fell, leaving

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:13.019
the United States with undisputed power.

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:17.709
During the Cold War, the United States built
an apparatus of military alliances and bases

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:19.910
to contain Soviet Communism.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:25.250
Once the Soviet enemy collapsed, America had
an opportunity to scale back its military

00:04:25.250 --> 00:04:31.830
footprint, invest the gains at home, and let
other countries manage their own affairs.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:37.540
But instead of pulling back, planners in the
Pentagon vowed to maintain a “predominant

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:43.450
military position” so great as to prevent
any potential rival from even “aspiring”

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:46.470
to a larger role in their regions.

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:51.870
No one would dare to take their own initiative
— not even U.S. allies, who would stay subordinate

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.940
to the United States.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:55.940
The result?

00:04:55.940 --> 00:05:01.920
Of all the times the United States has used
armed force since 1946, roughly 80 percent

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.830
of those interventions have taken place AFTER
1991.

00:05:05.830 --> 00:05:12.050
That’s right: the United States has used
force more frequently after the Cold War than

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:14.570
during it, and it’s not even close.

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:19.370
Despite the promise of “peace through strength,”
peace has NOT come.

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:20.760
Quite the contrary.

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:25.460
Armed supremacy, assumed to ensure peace,
has produced endless war.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:30.860
In the 1990s, the United States began to station
tens of thousands of soldiers in the Middle

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:31.960
East.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:37.990
Suddenly it found a CAVALCADE of enemies in
the region who resisted American aims.

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:39.940
Take Afghanistan.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:44.660
After 9/11, the United States did not merely
go after the terrorists responsible for the

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:47.360
attack and punish their sponsors.

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:50.170
Instead, America tried to transform Afghanistan.

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:56.320
It set out to build a brand new, Western-style
centralized state, applying what was supposed

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:00.130
to be overwhelming strength to impose peace.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:05.160
It soon became clear that, in the words of
the lieutenant general who for six years was

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:11.330
the White House’s chief advisor on the war,
quote, “we didn’t know what we were doing.”

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:16.800
The U.S. tried to build Afghan security forces,
only to find they couldn’t do the job.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.240
The U.S. tried to build a “thriving market
economy,” only to see the OPIUM TRADE become

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:24.060
the ONLY thriving part of the Afghan economy.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:29.800
The U.S. tried to build a liberal democracy,
but in doing so empowered warlords and fueled

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:30.800
corruption.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.850
So the Taliban mounted an insurgency and made
steady gains.

00:06:34.850 --> 00:06:40.150
Now the Taliban controls the country and enjoys
its strongest position since the war began

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:43.320
in 2001.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.420
War has come to appear normal, inescapable,
eternal.

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:52.270
It has no declared beginning, and no end in
sight.

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:57.440
That’s what makes them ENDLESS wars -- not
just that they are long, but also that they

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:00.940
lack a worthy and achievable purpose.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.600
The United States has trapped itself.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.800
Pursuing military dominance has generated
unnecessary enemies.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:12.350
These enemies, in turn, have made dominance
costlier and more dangerous.

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:17.690
That’s why year after year, the United States
seems to grow ever more threatened, and ever

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:22.690
less safe — according to the very leaders
RESPONSIBLE for that result!

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:28.530
It’s as though the most powerful nation
on earth has no control over its conduct — no

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:34.440
choice but to keep spending more on the military
than the next 11 countries COMBINED.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:39.860
As Americans struggle to address urgent needs
at home, their government has committed them

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:46.440
to the endless defense of places across the
world – places we often make MORE dangerous.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:52.210
Instead of strengthening ourselves and helping
to build a world safe for diversity, we’ve

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:55.950
chosen to build an empire, or something all
too close.

00:07:55.950 --> 00:08:01.800
And if we continue the quest for dominance,
the consequences will get even worse.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.130
And we will ALL be weaker for it.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:09.731
I’m Stephen Wertheim, Director of Grand
Strategy at the Quincy Institute, for the

00:08:09.731 --> 00:08:42.760
Gravel Institute.

